\chapter{Captured process streams and backpressure}
\label{ch:46}
\index{Captured process streams and backpressure}
\index{Processes!stream backpressure}

\OnxCodeTerm{launch capture} returns the process handle and three endpoints for standard input, standard output, and standard error. Input accepts bytes, while the output and error streams deliver bytes. Stream buffers are bounded. Closing input allows the child to see EOF once buffered writes drain. An output stream reaches EOF after the pipe closes and queued bytes have been consumed. NUL is a valid stream byte.

\section{Avoid a blocked producer}
If a child can fill stdout or stderr, arrange for the program to drain both during execution before relying on join. A blocked pipe writer can otherwise keep the child alive while the parent waits for exit. A focused test should send more than the configured buffer capacity, deliver input EOF, drain both outputs, and join exactly once. Mix byte streams and ordinary channels with \OnxCodeTerm{poll} when the parent must handle both. Keep process supervision failures distinct from empty stream chunks and ordinary EOF.

\section{Four bytes through cat}
The process-streams example sends 79, 110, 49 and 120 to cat, each explicitly typed as an unsigned byte. In ASCII these spell On1x. Its expected stdout observations, however, are four successful byte receives followed by a receive with no payload. The example converts each byte for printing rather than first building a string. Replacing 49 with zero would change the third numeric print to zero; neither the fourth byte nor the final no-payload observation should disappear. That single substitution is a good way to catch a consumer that has accidentally implemented C string termination.

Track whose statement makes progress at each stage. A successful input send means the adapter accepted a byte. It need not mean cat has already read the byte or written it back. The parent closes input after the fourth successful send; buffered input can still reach cat before it observes EOF. Cat copies the bytes to stdout, the parent drains them, and the parent can then observe stdout closure. On this successful path there is no stderr byte and the final join yields zero.

Could one use the same ordering to send a very large file and only then start reading? The four-byte example does not justify that generalization. Once the output queue and pipe fill, cat can stop consuming input while waiting for its write. The parent's next input send can then wait for space that only a reader on the output side can indirectly release. Arrange concurrent draining or interleave reads and writes for such a workload.

In particular, reading stdout to completion before starting on stderr is suitable for this successful cat example only because it has no error output to drain.

